// Class 11 Accountancy practice bank, part 2: Trial Balance and Rectification of Errors, Depreciation, Provisions and Reserves, Financial Statements I and II (chapters 6-9).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "accountancy", {
  "trial-balance-and-rectification-of-errors": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following accounts shows a credit balance in the trial balance?", options: ["Drawings A/c", "Sales returns A/c", "Discount allowed A/c", "Provision for doubtful debts A/c"], answer: 3, explain: "Provisions and reserves are liabilities-side items with credit balances. Drawings, sales returns and discount allowed all have **debit** balances.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The method most commonly used for preparing a trial balance is the:", options: ["Totals method", "Balance method", "Totals-and-balances method", "Journal method"], answer: 1, explain: "In the **balance method** only the balance of each ledger account is listed on its debit or credit side. It is the method generally used in practice.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following errors will NOT be disclosed by a trial balance?", options: ["Wrong totalling of the purchases book", "Posting an amount to the wrong side of an account", "A credit sale not recorded at all in the books", "Omitting to post a journal entry to one account"], answer: 2, explain: "A complete omission leaves debit and credit equal, so the trial balance still agrees. The other three affect only one side.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following pairs of errors is an example of compensating errors?", options: ["Sales book overcast by ₹1,000 and purchases book overcast by ₹1,000", "Sales book overcast by ₹1,000 and purchases book undercast by ₹1,000", "Goods sold to Ali not recorded and goods bought from Raj not recorded", "Rent paid debited to the landlord's account only"], answer: 0, explain: "Overcasting sales gives an excess credit of ₹1,000 and overcasting purchases gives an excess debit of ₹1,000; the two cancel, so the trial balance agrees. In the second pair both errors push in the same direction.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A transaction is correctly entered in the journal but is not posted to the ledger account concerned. This is an error of:", options: ["Commission", "Principle", "Compensation", "Partial omission"], answer: 3, explain: "A transaction recorded in the journal but left out of the ledger is an **error of (partial) omission**. It affects one side only, so the trial balance does not agree.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The credit side of a trial balance totals ₹1,200 more than the debit side. The suspense account will be shown:", options: ["On the credit side at ₹1,200", "On the credit side at ₹2,400", "On the debit side at ₹1,200", "On the debit side at ₹2,400"], answer: 2, explain: "To make the totals equal, the shortage on the **debit** side is filled by a suspense account on the debit side for the **difference of ₹1,200**, not for double the difference.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The sales book was overcast by ₹5,000, and the error was found after the trial balance was prepared. The rectifying entry is:", options: ["Suspense A/c Dr. ₹5,000 To Sales A/c ₹5,000", "Sales A/c Dr. ₹5,000 To Suspense A/c ₹5,000", "Sales A/c Dr. ₹10,000 To Suspense A/c ₹10,000", "Sales A/c Dr. ₹5,000 To Purchases A/c ₹5,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Overcasting gave Sales A/c an excess **credit** of ₹5,000. Debit Sales A/c to remove it, and credit Suspense A/c, which had been debited to balance the trial balance.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): If the trial balance agrees, the books are completely free from errors.\nReason (R): Errors of principle and compensating errors do not affect the agreement of the trial balance.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true: such errors affect both sides equally, so the totals still agree. A is false: agreement shows only arithmetical accuracy, not freedom from every error.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Purchase of a computer debited to Purchases A/c is an error of principle.\nReason (R): The error treats capital expenditure as revenue expenditure.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A computer is a fixed asset (capital expenditure) but was charged to a revenue account. This breaks accounting principles, and R states exactly why.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Kavita keeps the books of a stationery shop. At the year end she finds: (i) the purchase of a computer for ₹30,000 was debited to Purchases A/c; (ii) a credit sale of ₹2,100 to Rohit was posted to Rohit's account as ₹1,200.\nWhich error(s) will make the trial balance disagree?", options: ["Only (i)", "Only (ii)", "Both (i) and (ii)", "Both are hidden by the trial balance"], answer: 1, explain: "(i) is an error of principle: both the debit and the credit are correct in amount, so the totals agree. (ii) leaves the debit side ₹900 short, so the trial balance **disagrees**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "From the following balances the capital is: Purchases ₹40,000; sales returns ₹2,000; drawings ₹6,000; debtors ₹15,000; cash ₹3,000; furniture ₹10,000; salaries ₹8,000; sales ₹55,000; creditors ₹12,000; purchases returns ₹3,000; discount received ₹1,000. (The trial balance agrees.)", options: ["₹14,000", "₹71,000", "₹1,000", "₹13,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Debits = 40,000 + 2,000 + 6,000 + 15,000 + 3,000 + 10,000 + 8,000 = ₹84,000. Credits other than capital = 55,000 + 12,000 + 3,000 + 1,000 = ₹71,000. Capital = 84,000 − 71,000 = **₹13,000**. (₹14,000 leaves out discount received.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Goods sold on credit to Anil for ₹6,000 were wrongly entered in the purchases book. The rectifying entry debits Anil's A/c with:", options: ["₹12,000", "₹6,000", "₹18,000", "₹3,000"], answer: 0, explain: "The wrong entry credited Anil ₹6,000 instead of debiting him. Anil's A/c must be debited **₹12,000** (₹6,000 to cancel the wrong credit and ₹6,000 for the sale), with credit of ₹6,000 to Purchases A/c and ₹6,000 to Sales A/c.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A suspense account shows a debit balance of ₹6,000. Later it is found that (i) the sales book was overcast by ₹4,000 and (ii) rent of ₹900 was posted to the credit of Rent A/c instead of the debit. After these are rectified, the suspense account will have:", options: ["A ₹200 credit balance", "A ₹5,800 debit balance", "A ₹200 debit balance", "A ₹11,800 debit balance"], answer: 2, explain: "(i) credits suspense with ₹4,000. (ii) is a wrong-side posting, so Rent A/c is debited ₹1,800 (twice ₹900) and suspense is credited ₹1,800. Total credits = ₹5,800. Balance left = 6,000 − 5,800 = **₹200 debit**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A trader's books show a net profit of ₹1,00,000. The auditor finds: (i) the purchases book was overcast by ₹4,000; (ii) the sales book was undercast by ₹2,500; (iii) repairs of ₹1,500 were debited to Building A/c.\nThe corrected net profit is:", options: ["₹1,08,000", "₹1,05,000", "₹1,02,000", "₹92,000"], answer: 1, explain: "(i) Purchases overstated: profit understated, +₹4,000. (ii) Sales understated: +₹2,500. (iii) Repairs are an expense not charged: −₹1,500. Corrected profit = 1,00,000 + 4,000 + 2,500 − 1,500 = **₹1,05,000**.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a compensating error? Give one example.", a: "Compensating errors are two or more errors whose effects cancel each other so the trial balance still agrees. Example: Ram's account debited ₹200 short and Shyam's account debited ₹200 extra.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "On which side of the balance sheet is a debit balance of the suspense account shown if it cannot be cleared?", a: "On the **assets side** of the balance sheet. (A credit balance would be shown on the liabilities side.)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two objectives of preparing a trial balance.", a: "(1) To check the arithmetical accuracy of the ledger postings, since the debit and credit totals should agree.\n(2) To help in locating errors and to give a summary of ledger balances for preparing the financial statements.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Pass the rectifying entries: (i) Furniture bought for ₹6,000 was debited to Purchases A/c. (ii) Salary of ₹4,000 paid to an employee, Ravi, was debited to Ravi's personal account.", a: "(i) Furniture A/c Dr. ₹6,000\n       To Purchases A/c ₹6,000\n(Being furniture wrongly debited to Purchases, now corrected.)\n(ii) Salaries A/c Dr. ₹4,000\n       To Ravi's A/c ₹4,000\n(Being salary wrongly debited to Ravi's account, now corrected.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State with reasons whether each error is disclosed by the trial balance: (a) sales book undercast by ₹1,000; (b) goods returned by a customer not recorded at all; (c) cash received from Ali credited to Bilal's account.", a: "(a) **Disclosed**: only the credit side is short by ₹1,000, so the totals differ.\n(b) **Not disclosed**: neither the debit nor the credit was made, so both sides are still equal.\n(c) **Not disclosed**: the amount is posted on the correct (credit) side of a wrong account, so the totals still agree.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Pass rectifying entries (the errors were found after the trial balance was prepared and the difference was put to suspense): (i) The purchases book was undercast by ₹2,500. (ii) The total of the discount allowed column of the cash book, ₹300, was not posted to Discount Allowed A/c. (iii) Interest received ₹700 was credited to Interest Paid A/c.", a: "(i) Purchases A/c Dr. ₹2,500\n       To Suspense A/c ₹2,500\n(ii) Discount Allowed A/c Dr. ₹300\n       To Suspense A/c ₹300\n(iii) Interest Paid A/c Dr. ₹700\n       To Interest Received A/c ₹700\n(Entry (iii) is a two-sided error, so no suspense account is used.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A firm's net profit before correction was ₹48,000. Later it was found that (i) a credit sale of ₹5,000 to Mohan was not recorded at all; (ii) repairs of ₹2,000 were debited to Furniture A/c; (iii) the purchases book was overcast by ₹3,000. Find the corrected net profit.", a: "Net profit as per books                       ₹48,000\nAdd: (i) sale omitted                           ₹5,000\nAdd: (iii) purchases overstated            ₹3,000\nLess: (ii) repairs not charged                (₹2,000)\nCorrected net profit = 48,000 + 5,000 + 3,000 − 2,000 = **₹54,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prepare the trial balance of Rajesh Traders as on 31 March 2025 from these balances (₹): capital 2,00,000; drawings 18,000; furniture 30,000; machinery 1,20,000; opening stock 25,000; purchases 1,60,000; sales 2,05,000; sales returns 4,000; purchases returns 5,000; wages 12,000; rent 9,000; salaries 24,000; debtors 45,000; creditors 48,000; cash 6,000; bank balance 20,000; commission received 7,000; bank loan 8,000.", a: "Trial Balance of Rajesh Traders as on 31 March 2025\nParticulars                          Dr (₹)        Cr (₹)\nCapital                                  –         2,00,000\nDrawings                           18,000              –\nFurniture                          30,000              –\nMachinery                       1,20,000              –\nOpening stock                    25,000              –\nPurchases                       1,60,000              –\nSales                                    –         2,05,000\nSales returns                       4,000              –\nPurchases returns                   –              5,000\nWages                              12,000              –\nRent                                 9,000              –\nSalaries                           24,000              –\nDebtors                            45,000              –\nCreditors                              –            48,000\nCash                                 6,000              –\nBank balance                    20,000              –\nCommission received                 –              7,000\nBank loan                              –              8,000\nTotal                           4,73,000      4,73,000\nBoth totals are ₹4,73,000, so the trial balance agrees. (Assets, expenses, drawings, stock, sales returns have debit balances; capital, liabilities, incomes, sales, purchases returns have credit balances.)", marks: 5 },
      { q: "The trial balance of a firm did not agree and the difference was put to suspense. The following errors were found later: (i) the sales book was overcast by ₹5,000; (ii) purchases returns of ₹1,200 were posted to the debit of Purchases Returns A/c; (iii) cash received from Mohan, ₹3,000, was recorded in the cash book but not posted to Mohan's A/c; (iv) machinery bought for ₹15,000 was debited to Purchases A/c; (v) rent paid ₹800 was posted to Rent A/c as ₹80. Pass the rectifying entries and prepare the Suspense A/c to find the original difference.", a: "(i) Sales A/c Dr. ₹5,000\n       To Suspense A/c ₹5,000\n(ii) Suspense A/c Dr. ₹2,400\n       To Purchases Returns A/c ₹2,400  (twice ₹1,200)\n(iii) Suspense A/c Dr. ₹3,000\n       To Mohan's A/c ₹3,000\n(iv) Machinery A/c Dr. ₹15,000\n       To Purchases A/c ₹15,000  (no suspense: two-sided error)\n(v) Rent A/c Dr. ₹720\n       To Suspense A/c ₹720  (800 − 80)\n\nSuspense Account\nDr:  To Difference in trial balance (balancing figure)  320\n     To Purchases Returns A/c  2,400\n     To Mohan's A/c  3,000          Total  5,720\nCr:  By Sales A/c  5,000\n     By Rent A/c  720                Total  5,720\nThe trial balance had a **credit** side excess of ₹320, shown as a debit of ₹320 in suspense; the account closes to nil.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The trial balance of Anil Stores showed debit total ₹5,41,300 and credit total ₹5,45,100. The difference was put to a suspense account. Later it was found that (a) the sales book was overcast by ₹5,000 and (b) the purchases book was overcast by ₹1,200.\n(i) Show the suspense account's side and amount in the trial balance.\n(ii) Pass the rectifying entries.\n(iii) What is the balance of the suspense account after the rectification?", a: "(i) Credit exceeds debit by 5,45,100 − 5,41,300 = ₹3,800, so Suspense A/c ₹3,800 appears on the **debit** side of the trial balance.\n(ii) (a) Sales A/c Dr. ₹5,000 To Suspense A/c ₹5,000\n(b) Suspense A/c Dr. ₹1,200 To Purchases A/c ₹1,200\n(iii) Suspense: Dr 3,800 + 1,200 = 5,000; Cr 5,000. The account **closes with nil balance**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "depreciation-provisions-and-reserves": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Depreciation is a:", options: ["Cash expense", "Non-cash expense", "Capital receipt", "Revenue receipt"], answer: 1, explain: "Charging depreciation does not involve any outflow of cash; it only spreads the cost of the asset over its life. It is a **non-cash expense**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Writing off the cost of a patent over its useful life is called:", options: ["Depletion", "Obsolescence", "Amortisation", "Appreciation"], answer: 2, explain: "**Amortisation** is used for intangible assets such as patents, copyrights and trademarks. Depletion applies to natural resources.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which method is generally suitable for assets such as leases and patents, whose benefit is spread evenly over their life?", options: ["Straight line method", "Written down value method", "Depletion method", "Machine hour method"], answer: 0, explain: "Their use is spread evenly over time, so an equal charge every year, the **straight line method**, suits them.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A capital reserve is created out of:", options: ["Operating profits of the year", "Provisions for liabilities", "Undervalued assets", "Capital profits"], answer: 3, explain: "A capital reserve comes from **capital profits** such as profit on revaluation of assets, not from the normal operating profit.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The depreciable cost of an asset is:", options: ["Cost + scrap value", "Cost − scrap value", "Cost × useful life", "Cost ÷ useful life"], answer: 1, explain: "Depreciable cost = Cost − Estimated net residual (scrap) value. It is the amount to be written off over the asset's useful life.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Machinery was bought on 1 July 2024 for ₹1,20,000 with no scrap value. Depreciation is charged by the straight line method at 10% p.a. on cost, and the books close on 31 March. Depreciation for 2024-25 is:", options: ["₹12,000", "₹6,000", "₹9,000", "₹10,000"], answer: 2, explain: "The asset was used for 9 months: 1,20,000 × 10/100 × 9/12 = **₹9,000**. (₹12,000 charges a full year.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A machine costing ₹60,000 is depreciated at 20% p.a. by the written down value method. Depreciation for the third year is:", options: ["₹7,680", "₹9,600", "₹12,000", "₹8,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Year 1: 12,000, book value 48,000. Year 2: 9,600, book value 38,400. Year 3: 20% of 38,400 = **₹7,680**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Under the written down value method, an asset can never be completely written off.\nReason (R): Depreciation is a fixed percentage of the opening book value, which keeps reducing.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A percentage of a falling balance never reaches zero, so the book value never becomes nil. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A secret reserve does not appear in the balance sheet.\nReason (R): A secret reserve is created by overvaluing assets or undervaluing liabilities.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true, since the reserve is hidden. R is false: a secret reserve is created by **undervaluing** assets or **overvaluing** liabilities.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Sunita buys a delivery van for ₹5,00,000 on 1 April 2023. Its estimated life is 5 years and the scrap value is ₹50,000. She uses the straight line method.\nThe book value of the van on 31 March 2025 is:", options: ["₹3,60,000", "₹3,20,000", "₹3,70,000", "₹4,10,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Annual depreciation = (5,00,000 − 50,000) ÷ 5 = ₹90,000. For two years = ₹1,80,000. Book value = 5,00,000 − 1,80,000 = **₹3,20,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A machine costing ₹2,00,000 was bought on 1 April 2022 and is depreciated by the straight line method at 10% p.a. on cost (no scrap value). It was sold on 31 March 2025 for ₹1,30,000. The result of the sale is:", options: ["Loss of ₹10,000", "Profit of ₹10,000", "Loss of ₹70,000", "Profit of ₹70,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Depreciation for 3 years = 3 × 20,000 = ₹60,000. Book value = 1,40,000. Sale price 1,30,000 is less, so loss = **₹10,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A machine is listed at ₹3,00,000. A 10% trade discount is allowed, freight is ₹8,000, installation wages are ₹12,000 and annual repairs are ₹5,000. Life is 10 years and scrap value is ₹20,000. Annual depreciation by the straight line method is:", options: ["₹29,000", "₹27,000", "₹27,500", "₹28,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Cost = 2,70,000 + 8,000 + 12,000 = ₹2,90,000 (repairs are revenue expenditure and excluded). Depreciation = (2,90,000 − 20,000) ÷ 10 = **₹27,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Machinery bought on 1 April 2023 for ₹1,00,000 is depreciated at 10% p.a. by the written down value method (books close on 31 March). It was sold on 1 October 2024 for ₹80,000. The result of the sale is:", options: ["Profit of ₹5,000", "Loss of ₹10,000", "Profit of ₹5,500", "Loss of ₹5,500"], answer: 3, explain: "2023-24: depreciation ₹10,000, book value ₹90,000. 2024-25 (6 months): 90,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = ₹4,500, book value ₹85,500 on the date of sale. Loss = 85,500 − 80,000 = **₹5,500**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A firm bought furniture for ₹50,000 on 1 April 2022 and charges depreciation at 10% p.a. by the written down value method, closing its books on 31 March.\nThe total depreciation charged for the three years ended 31 March 2025 is:", options: ["₹13,550", "₹15,000", "₹13,500", "₹14,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Year 1: 5,000 (book value 45,000). Year 2: 4,500 (40,500). Year 3: 4,050 (36,450). Total = 5,000 + 4,500 + 4,050 = **₹13,550**.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define depreciation.", a: "Depreciation is the permanent, gradual and continuous fall in the value of a fixed asset due to use, passage of time or obsolescence.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is depreciation called a non-cash expense?", a: "Because it is charged against profit but no cash is paid out when it is charged; it only writes off part of the asset's cost.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two causes of depreciation.", a: "(1) **Wear and tear** from use of the asset.\n(2) **Obsolescence** due to new technology or change in demand. (Others: expiry of legal rights, abnormal factors such as accidents.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a revenue reserve and a capital reserve (any two points).", a: "(1) A **revenue reserve** is created out of operating (revenue) profits, e.g. general reserve; a **capital reserve** is created out of capital profits, e.g. profit on revaluation of assets.\n(2) A revenue reserve can be used for any purpose, including paying dividends; a capital reserve is generally not used for distributing dividends.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A machine was purchased for ₹2,50,000 and ₹30,000 was spent on its installation. Its life is 10 years and scrap value ₹20,000. Under the straight line method, calculate the annual depreciation and the book value at the end of the 4th year.", a: "Cost = 2,50,000 + 30,000 = ₹2,80,000.\nAnnual depreciation = (2,80,000 − 20,000) ÷ 10 = **₹26,000**.\nDepreciation for 4 years = 4 × 26,000 = ₹1,04,000.\nBook value after 4 years = 2,80,000 − 1,04,000 = **₹1,76,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Machinery was purchased on 1 April 2022 for ₹80,000. Depreciation is charged at 10% p.a. by the written down value method, directly to the asset account. Show the Machinery Account for the three years ended 31 March 2025.", a: "Machinery Account\n2022-23\nDr: 1 Apr 2022 To Bank 80,000\nCr: 31 Mar 2023 By Depreciation 8,000; By Balance c/d 72,000   (total 80,000)\n2023-24\nDr: To Balance b/d 72,000\nCr: By Depreciation 7,200; By Balance c/d 64,800   (total 72,000)\n2024-25\nDr: To Balance b/d 64,800\nCr: By Depreciation 6,480; By Balance c/d 58,320   (total 64,800)\nBook value on 31 March 2025 is **₹58,320**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Pass journal entries for depreciation of ₹24,000 on machinery for the year (i) when depreciation is charged directly to the Machinery Account, and (ii) when a Provision for Depreciation Account is maintained. Include the transfer to the Profit and Loss Account.", a: "(i) Depreciation A/c Dr. ₹24,000\n       To Machinery A/c ₹24,000\n    Profit and Loss A/c Dr. ₹24,000\n       To Depreciation A/c ₹24,000\n(ii) Depreciation A/c Dr. ₹24,000\n       To Provision for Depreciation A/c ₹24,000\n    Profit and Loss A/c Dr. ₹24,000\n       To Depreciation A/c ₹24,000\nIn (i) the machinery is reduced to book value; in (ii) it stays at original cost.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Machinery was bought on 1 April 2022 for ₹2,00,000. Depreciation is charged by the straight line method at 10% p.a. on cost (no scrap value) through a Provision for Depreciation Account. On 31 March 2025 the machinery was sold for ₹1,25,000. Prepare the Machinery Account, the Provision for Depreciation Account and the Asset Disposal Account.", a: "Machinery Account (2022-25)\nDr: 1 Apr 2022 To Bank 2,00,000\nCr: 31 Mar 2025 By Asset Disposal A/c 2,00,000\n\nProvision for Depreciation Account\nCr: 31 Mar 2023 By Depreciation 20,000; 31 Mar 2024 By Depreciation 20,000; 31 Mar 2025 By Depreciation 20,000   (total 60,000)\nDr: 31 Mar 2025 To Asset Disposal A/c 60,000\n\nAsset Disposal Account\nDr: To Machinery A/c 2,00,000\nCr: By Provision for Depreciation A/c 60,000; By Bank 1,25,000; By Profit and Loss A/c (loss) 15,000   (total 2,00,000)\nBook value = 2,00,000 − 60,000 = ₹1,40,000; sale price ₹1,25,000, so **loss on sale = ₹15,000**, transferred to Profit and Loss A/c.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A machine was bought on 1 July 2023 for ₹2,40,000. Depreciation is charged at 10% p.a. by the written down value method, directly to the asset account, and the books close on 31 March. The machine was sold on 1 October 2025 for ₹1,80,000. Calculate the depreciation for each period and the profit or loss on sale.", a: "2023-24 (9 months): 2,40,000 × 10/100 × 9/12 = ₹18,000. Book value = ₹2,22,000.\n2024-25: 10% of 2,22,000 = ₹22,200. Book value = ₹1,99,800.\n2025-26 (1 April to 1 October, 6 months): 1,99,800 × 10/100 × 6/12 = ₹9,990. Book value on sale = 1,99,800 − 9,990 = ₹1,89,810.\nSale price = ₹1,80,000.\n**Loss on sale = 1,89,810 − 1,80,000 = ₹9,810**, debited to Profit and Loss A/c.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Mehta Traders earned a profit of ₹3,00,000 before the following items. It charged depreciation of ₹15,000 on machinery, created a provision for doubtful debts of ₹8,000, and transferred ₹50,000 to general reserve.\n(i) Which of these are charges against profit and which is an appropriation of profit?\n(ii) Why must the provision be made even if the firm has no profit?\n(iii) Find the profit left after all three items.", a: "(i) Depreciation and the provision for doubtful debts are **charges against profit**; the transfer to general reserve is an **appropriation of profit**.\n(ii) A provision is for a known liability or expected loss, so it must be made whether or not there is a profit; a reserve is made only out of profit.\n(iii) 3,00,000 − 15,000 − 8,000 = ₹2,77,000; after the reserve of ₹50,000, profit left = **₹2,27,000**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "financial-statements-1": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Gross profit is the excess of:", options: ["Cost of goods sold over net sales", "Net sales over cost of goods sold", "Sales over purchases", "Net profit over indirect expenses"], answer: 1, explain: "Gross profit = Net sales − Cost of goods sold. Using plain purchases instead of the cost of goods sold ignores stock and direct expenses.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a revenue expenditure?", options: ["Purchase of a delivery van", "Installation of a new generator", "Legal fees for buying land", "Repairs to office furniture"], answer: 3, explain: "Repairs only maintain an existing asset and benefit the current year, so they are **revenue expenditure**. The other three add to the cost of fixed assets.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the balance sheet of a sole proprietor, the net profit of the year is:", options: ["Shown on the assets side", "Deducted from capital", "Credited to Sales A/c", "Added to capital"], answer: 3, explain: "Net profit increases the owner's claim, so it is **added to capital** (a net loss is deducted).", level: 1 },
      { q: "When assets are arranged in the order of liquidity, the first item shown is:", options: ["Land and buildings", "Goodwill", "Cash in hand", "Furniture"], answer: 2, explain: "Under the order of liquidity the **most liquid** asset, cash, comes first, and the least liquid such as goodwill and land comes last.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a direct expense, debited to the trading account?", options: ["Fuel and power used in production", "Office salaries", "Advertising", "Discount allowed"], answer: 0, explain: "Fuel and power used in production is a direct expense and goes to the trading account. The others are indirect expenses charged in the profit and loss account.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Opening stock ₹20,000; purchases ₹1,20,000; purchases returns ₹5,000; carriage inwards ₹3,000; closing stock ₹18,000. The cost of goods sold is:", options: ["₹1,25,000", "₹1,17,000", "₹1,56,000", "₹1,20,000"], answer: 3, explain: "20,000 + (1,20,000 − 5,000) + 3,000 − 18,000 = **₹1,20,000**. (₹1,25,000 ignores the purchases returns; ₹1,17,000 ignores carriage inwards.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "Sales ₹2,10,000; sales returns ₹10,000; cost of goods sold ₹1,20,000. The gross profit is:", options: ["₹90,000", "₹80,000", "₹70,000", "₹2,00,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Net sales = 2,10,000 − 10,000 = ₹2,00,000. Gross profit = 2,00,000 − 1,20,000 = **₹80,000**. (₹90,000 forgets the sales returns.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Carriage outwards is debited to the profit and loss account.\nReason (R): It is an indirect expense incurred in delivering goods to customers after they are ready for sale.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Carriage outwards is a selling expense, i.e. an indirect expense, so it is charged in the profit and loss account. R gives the reason.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Loss of goods by fire is deducted while calculating operating profit from net profit.\nReason (R): Operating profit excludes non-operating items such as losses by fire and profit on sale of assets.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. A is false: the loss by fire has already reduced net profit, so to reach operating profit it is **added back**, not deducted.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Ritu buys a second-hand scooter for ₹40,000 for her shop's deliveries. She pays ₹5,000 to repair it before use, ₹1,500 for petrol and ₹3,000 for its annual insurance.\nThe amount to be capitalised as the cost of the scooter is:", options: ["₹40,000", "₹46,500", "₹45,000", "₹49,500"], answer: 2, explain: "Repairs needed to bring the asset to working condition are part of its cost: 40,000 + 5,000 = **₹45,000**. Petrol and insurance are revenue expenses.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Opening capital ₹5,00,000; additional capital introduced ₹50,000; net profit ₹1,20,000; drawings ₹60,000. The closing capital is:", options: ["₹6,10,000", "₹5,70,000", "₹7,30,000", "₹6,70,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Closing capital = 5,00,000 + 50,000 + 1,20,000 − 60,000 = **₹6,10,000**. (₹6,70,000 forgets to deduct drawings.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Net profit is ₹1,50,000 after including interest on investments ₹6,000 and profit on sale of furniture ₹10,000, and after deducting a loss by fire of ₹8,000. The operating profit is:", options: ["₹1,58,000", "₹1,26,000", "₹1,42,000", "₹1,74,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Operating profit = 1,50,000 − 6,000 − 10,000 + 8,000 = **₹1,42,000** (remove non-operating incomes, add back the non-operating loss).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Gross profit ₹90,000; salaries ₹30,000; rent ₹12,000; discount allowed ₹2,000; interest paid ₹4,000; commission received ₹5,000. The net profit is:", options: ["₹42,000", "₹47,000", "₹52,000", "₹38,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Expenses = 30,000 + 12,000 + 2,000 + 4,000 = ₹48,000. Net profit = 90,000 + 5,000 − 48,000 = **₹47,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: At the end of the year a trader's assets total ₹6,00,000 and his liabilities to outsiders are ₹1,50,000. During the year he introduced ₹30,000 of fresh capital, withdrew ₹40,000 and earned a net profit of ₹1,00,000.\nHis capital at the beginning of the year was:", options: ["₹2,80,000", "₹4,50,000", "₹4,20,000", "₹3,60,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Closing capital = 6,00,000 − 1,50,000 = ₹4,50,000. Opening capital = 4,50,000 − 1,00,000 − 30,000 + 40,000 = **₹3,60,000**.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define gross profit.", a: "Gross profit is the excess of net sales over the cost of goods sold. It is the balance of the trading account.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give one example of deferred revenue expenditure.", a: "A heavy advertising campaign for launching a new product, whose benefit lasts for several years and is therefore written off over those years.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two objectives of preparing financial statements.", a: "(1) To find the **profit or loss** of the business for the accounting period.\n(2) To show the **financial position** (assets, liabilities and capital) of the business on the last day of the period.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State any two differences between the trading account and the profit and loss account.", a: "(1) The trading account finds the **gross profit**; the profit and loss account finds the **net profit**.\n(2) The trading account shows opening stock, purchases and direct expenses; the profit and loss account shows indirect expenses (salaries, rent, advertising) and other incomes.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Pass the closing entries for these year-end balances: opening stock ₹10,000; purchases ₹70,000; wages ₹8,000; sales ₹1,25,000; closing stock ₹15,000.", a: "(1) Trading A/c Dr. ₹88,000\n       To Opening Stock A/c ₹10,000\n       To Purchases A/c ₹70,000\n       To Wages A/c ₹8,000\n(2) Sales A/c Dr. ₹1,25,000\n       To Trading A/c ₹1,25,000\n(3) Closing Stock A/c Dr. ₹15,000\n       To Trading A/c ₹15,000\n(4) Trading A/c Dr. ₹52,000\n       To Profit and Loss A/c ₹52,000 (gross profit)\nCheck: debit side 88,000 + 52,000 = 1,40,000 = credit side 1,25,000 + 15,000.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A trader's net profit is ₹82,000. It is after charging a loss by fire of ₹3,000 and includes interest on investments ₹5,000 and profit on sale of old furniture ₹4,000. Calculate the operating profit.", a: "Net profit                                              ₹82,000\nAdd: non-operating loss (loss by fire)         ₹3,000\nLess: interest on investments                  (₹5,000)\nLess: profit on sale of furniture              (₹4,000)\nOperating profit = 82,000 + 3,000 − 5,000 − 4,000 = **₹76,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A proprietor's opening capital was ₹3,00,000. He introduced ₹40,000 more, withdrew ₹55,000 and the year's net profit was ₹90,000. Show how the capital is presented in the balance sheet.", a: "Capital as on 1 April                       ₹3,00,000\nAdd: additional capital introduced             ₹40,000\nAdd: net profit for the year                       ₹90,000\n                                                          ₹4,30,000\nLess: drawings                                          ₹55,000\nCapital at the end of the year = **₹3,75,000**, shown on the liabilities side.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "From the following trial balance extract and information, prepare the trading and profit and loss account for the year ended 31 March 2025 and the balance sheet as on that date. Trial balance (₹): opening stock 20,000; purchases 1,50,000; wages 10,000; carriage inwards 4,000; salaries 24,000; rent 6,000; carriage outwards 3,000; sales returns 5,000; drawings 12,000; furniture 30,000; debtors 40,000; cash 8,000; bank 17,000 (all debit); capital 84,000; sales 2,10,000; purchases returns 7,000; creditors 25,000; commission received 3,000 (all credit). Closing stock is ₹30,000.", a: "Trading Account for the year ended 31 March 2025\nDr: Opening stock 20,000; Purchases 1,50,000 less returns 7,000 = 1,43,000; Wages 10,000; Carriage inwards 4,000; Gross profit c/d 58,000   Total 2,35,000\nCr: Sales 2,10,000 less returns 5,000 = 2,05,000; Closing stock 30,000   Total 2,35,000\n\nProfit and Loss Account\nDr: Salaries 24,000; Rent 6,000; Carriage outwards 3,000; Net profit 28,000   Total 61,000\nCr: Gross profit b/d 58,000; Commission received 3,000   Total 61,000\n\nBalance Sheet as on 31 March 2025\nLiabilities: Capital 84,000 + net profit 28,000 − drawings 12,000 = 1,00,000; Creditors 25,000   Total 1,25,000\nAssets: Cash 8,000; Bank 17,000; Debtors 40,000; Closing stock 30,000; Furniture 30,000   Total 1,25,000\nBoth sides of each statement agree.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A trader's records show: opening stock ₹40,000; purchases ₹2,60,000; purchases returns ₹10,000; direct expenses ₹20,000; sales ₹4,10,000; sales returns ₹10,000; closing stock ₹50,000; indirect expenses ₹60,000; interest on investments received ₹8,000; profit on sale of an old machine ₹6,000; loss by fire ₹5,000; opening capital ₹3,00,000; drawings ₹30,000. Calculate (i) cost of goods sold, (ii) gross profit, (iii) net profit, (iv) operating profit, (v) closing capital.", a: "(i) Cost of goods sold = 40,000 + (2,60,000 − 10,000) + 20,000 − 50,000 = **₹2,60,000**.\n(ii) Net sales = 4,10,000 − 10,000 = ₹4,00,000. Gross profit = 4,00,000 − 2,60,000 = **₹1,40,000**.\n(iii) Net profit = 1,40,000 + 8,000 + 6,000 − 60,000 − 5,000 = **₹89,000**.\n(iv) Operating profit = 89,000 − 8,000 − 6,000 + 5,000 = **₹80,000** (this equals gross profit − indirect expenses).\n(v) Closing capital = 3,00,000 + 89,000 − 30,000 = **₹3,59,000**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Ravi runs a general store. During the year he paid ₹1,20,000 for a new refrigerator, ₹6,000 as freight to bring it to the shop, ₹4,500 as carriage on goods purchased and ₹2,000 for repairing the shop shutter. He also received ₹15,000 by selling his old refrigerator.\n(i) At what amount should the new refrigerator be capitalised?\n(ii) Which of the payments is debited to the trading account?\n(iii) Classify the repairs and the sale proceeds of the old refrigerator.", a: "(i) Cost = 1,20,000 + 6,000 freight = **₹1,26,000** (freight to bring an asset to the business is capital expenditure).\n(ii) The carriage of ₹4,500 on goods purchased (carriage inwards) is a direct expense debited to the **trading account**.\n(iii) Shutter repairs of ₹2,000 are **revenue expenditure** (profit and loss account); the ₹15,000 from selling the old refrigerator is a **capital receipt**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "financial-statements-2": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Accrued income is shown in the balance sheet as a:", options: ["Current liability", "Current asset", "Fixed asset", "Deduction from capital"], answer: 1, explain: "Accrued income is earned but not yet received, so the business has a right to receive it: a **current asset**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the final accounts of a sole proprietor, interest on drawings is treated as:", options: ["An addition to capital", "An expense debited to profit and loss account", "A direct expense", "An income credited to profit and loss account"], answer: 3, explain: "Interest charged on drawings is an **income** of the business, credited to the profit and loss account, and the proprietor's capital is reduced by it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Further bad debts given in the adjustments are shown in the balance sheet as a deduction from:", options: ["Creditors", "Capital", "Debtors", "Closing stock"], answer: 2, explain: "Further bad debts are irrecoverable, so they are deducted from **sundry debtors** (and also added to bad debts in the profit and loss account).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Interest on the proprietor's capital is:", options: ["Debited to profit and loss account and added to capital", "Credited to profit and loss account and deducted from capital", "Debited to trading account and added to drawings", "Credited to profit and loss account and added to capital"], answer: 0, explain: "It is an expense of the business, so it is **debited to profit and loss**, and it increases the proprietor's capital.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A prepaid expense, given as an adjustment, is dealt with by:", options: ["Adding it to the expense", "Deducting it from the expense", "Ignoring it completely", "Showing it as a current liability"], answer: 1, explain: "Only the part of the expense belonging to the current year is charged, so the prepaid amount is **deducted** from the expense and shown as a current asset.", level: 1 },
      { q: "An insurance premium of ₹18,000 was paid on 1 October 2024 for 12 months. For the year ended 31 March 2025, the expense charged and the prepaid insurance are:", options: ["₹18,000 and nil", "₹6,000 and prepaid ₹12,000", "₹9,000 and prepaid ₹9,000", "₹12,000 and prepaid ₹6,000"], answer: 2, explain: "October to March is 6 months: 18,000 × 6/12 = ₹9,000 is the expense; the remaining 6 months, **₹9,000**, are prepaid.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Debtors are ₹60,000 and further bad debts of ₹2,000 are to be written off. A provision for doubtful debts at 5% is to be created. The new provision is:", options: ["₹3,000", "₹2,950", "₹3,100", "₹2,900"], answer: 3, explain: "The provision is calculated on debtors after further bad debts: (60,000 − 2,000) × 5/100 = **₹2,900**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The provision for doubtful debts is calculated on debtors after deducting further bad debts.\nReason (R): Further bad debts are already irrecoverable, so they are no longer debtors.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A debt that has been written off cannot also be provided for. R gives the reason for A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Interest on drawings is an income for the business.\nReason (R): Interest on drawings is added to the proprietor's capital in the balance sheet.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: the business charges it to the proprietor and credits profit and loss. R is false: it is **deducted** from capital, not added.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Mohit sub-lets a room of his shop. On 1 January 2025 he received ₹36,000 as rent for 12 months starting that day. His books close on 31 March 2025.\nThe rent income to be credited to the profit and loss account for 2024-25 is:", options: ["₹9,000", "₹36,000", "₹27,000", "₹3,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Rent per month = ₹3,000. January to March is 3 months = **₹9,000**. The remaining ₹27,000 is income received in advance (a current liability).", level: 2 },
      { q: "The trial balance shows debtors ₹52,000, bad debts ₹3,000 and provision for doubtful debts ₹5,000. Further bad debts are ₹2,000 and the provision is to be kept at 6% on debtors. The amount charged to profit and loss for bad debts and provision is:", options: ["₹8,000", "₹5,000", "₹3,000", "₹3,120"], answer: 2, explain: "New provision = 6% of (52,000 − 2,000) = ₹3,000. Charge = 3,000 + 2,000 + 3,000 − 5,000 = **₹3,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A manager is entitled to 5% commission on net profit after charging such commission. The net profit before the commission is ₹2,10,000. The commission is:", options: ["₹10,500", "₹10,000", "₹11,000", "₹9,500"], answer: 1, explain: "Commission = 2,10,000 × 5 ÷ 105 = **₹10,000**. Check: profit after commission = ₹2,00,000 and 5% of it is ₹10,000. (₹10,500 is 5% of the profit before commission.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A proprietor's capital at the start of the year was ₹3,00,000, and he introduced ₹60,000 more on 1 October. Interest on capital is allowed at 10% p.a. Interest on drawings charged to him is ₹1,200. These two adjustments change the net profit by:", options: ["Reduce it by ₹34,200", "Increase it by ₹31,800", "Reduce it by ₹33,000", "Reduce it by ₹31,800"], answer: 3, explain: "Interest on capital = 30,000 + 60,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = ₹33,000, an expense. Interest on drawings ₹1,200 is an income. Net reduction = 33,000 − 1,200 = **₹31,800**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A fire destroyed stock costing ₹60,000 in a trader's godown. The insurance company admitted a claim of ₹45,000.\nThe amount debited to the profit and loss account as loss by fire is:", options: ["₹60,000", "₹15,000", "₹45,000", "₹1,05,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Stock lost (₹60,000) is credited to the trading account. The claim of ₹45,000 is a current asset, and the uninsured loss, 60,000 − 45,000 = **₹15,000**, is debited to profit and loss.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is accrued income? Give one example.", a: "Accrued income is income earned during the year but not yet received. Example: interest on investments for the last few months that is still due.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Where is prepaid insurance shown in the final accounts?", a: "It is deducted from insurance in the profit and loss account and shown as a **current asset** in the balance sheet.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Outstanding salaries of ₹5,000 are given as an adjustment (not in the trial balance). State their treatment in the final accounts.", a: "(1) Added to salaries on the debit side of the profit and loss account, so that salaries of the whole year are charged.\n(2) Shown as a **current liability** (outstanding salaries) in the balance sheet.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give the two-fold effect of (a) prepaid insurance and (b) income received in advance.", a: "(a) Prepaid insurance: **deducted** from insurance in the profit and loss account; shown as a **current asset**.\n(b) Income received in advance: **deducted** from the income on the credit side of the profit and loss account; shown as a **current liability**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The trial balance shows debtors ₹80,000, bad debts ₹2,000 and provision for doubtful debts ₹4,000. Further bad debts are ₹3,000 and the provision is to be 5% on debtors. Find the amount charged to profit and loss and show debtors in the balance sheet.", a: "Debtors after further bad debts = 80,000 − 3,000 = ₹77,000.\nNew provision = 5% of 77,000 = ₹3,850.\nCharge to profit and loss = 2,000 + 3,000 + 3,850 − 4,000 = **₹4,850**.\nBalance sheet: Debtors 80,000 − further bad debts 3,000 − provision 3,850 = **₹73,150**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show the treatment in the profit and loss account and balance sheet: (i) wages in the trial balance ₹60,000, outstanding wages ₹5,000; (ii) rent paid ₹24,000, including ₹4,000 paid in advance; (iii) commission received ₹9,000, commission accrued ₹1,500.", a: "(i) Wages: 60,000 + 5,000 = ₹65,000 (debit side of P&L). Outstanding wages ₹5,000: current liability.\n(ii) Rent: 24,000 − 4,000 = ₹20,000 (debit side of P&L). Prepaid rent ₹4,000: current asset.\n(iii) Commission: 9,000 + 1,500 = ₹10,500 (credit side of P&L). Accrued commission ₹1,500: current asset.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The net profit of a firm before the manager's commission is ₹1,32,000. The manager is entitled to 10% commission. Calculate the commission (a) on the profit before charging such commission and (b) on the profit after charging such commission.", a: "(a) Before charging: 1,32,000 × 10/100 = **₹13,200**.\n(b) After charging: 1,32,000 × 10 ÷ 110 = **₹12,000**.\nCheck for (b): profit after commission = 1,32,000 − 12,000 = ₹1,20,000, and 10% of ₹1,20,000 = ₹12,000.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "From the following trial balance (31 March 2025) and adjustments, prepare the trading and profit and loss account and the balance sheet. Debit (₹): opening stock 25,000; purchases 2,00,000; wages 20,000; salaries 30,000; rent 12,000; insurance 6,000; bad debts 1,000; furniture 40,000; debtors 50,000; cash 4,000; drawings 20,000. Credit (₹): capital 80,000; sales 2,80,000; creditors 40,000; provision for doubtful debts 2,000; commission received 6,000. Adjustments: (i) closing stock ₹35,000; (ii) outstanding wages ₹3,000; (iii) prepaid insurance ₹1,500; (iv) further bad debts ₹2,000 and provision for doubtful debts to be maintained at 5% on debtors; (v) depreciate furniture at 10%; (vi) interest on capital at 5%.", a: "Trading Account\nDr: Opening stock 25,000; Purchases 2,00,000; Wages 20,000 + 3,000 = 23,000; Gross profit 67,000   Total 3,15,000\nCr: Sales 2,80,000; Closing stock 35,000   Total 3,15,000\n\nProfit and Loss Account\nDr: Salaries 30,000; Rent 12,000; Insurance 6,000 − 1,500 = 4,500; Bad debts 1,000 + 2,000 = 3,000; Provision for doubtful debts (new 2,400 − old 2,000) 400; Depreciation 4,000; Interest on capital 4,000; Net profit 15,100   Total 73,000\nCr: Gross profit 67,000; Commission received 6,000   Total 73,000\n\nBalance Sheet as on 31 March 2025\nLiabilities: Capital 80,000 + interest 4,000 + net profit 15,100 − drawings 20,000 = 79,100; Creditors 40,000; Outstanding wages 3,000   Total 1,22,100\nAssets: Cash 4,000; Debtors 50,000 − 2,000 − provision 2,400 = 45,600; Prepaid insurance 1,500; Closing stock 35,000; Furniture 40,000 − 4,000 = 36,000   Total 1,22,100\nNew provision = 5% of (50,000 − 2,000) = 2,400. Both sides of each statement agree.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "For the year ended 31 March 2025, show the amount charged or credited in the profit and loss account and the balance sheet figure for each: (i) salaries paid ₹1,80,000 (including ₹10,000 for the previous year), with ₹15,000 for the last month unpaid; (ii) rent received ₹60,000, of which ₹12,000 relates to next year; (iii) interest on investments received ₹8,000, with ₹2,000 accrued; (iv) insurance premium ₹14,400 paid on 1 January 2025 for one year; (v) machinery ₹1,60,000, to be depreciated at 15%.", a: "(i) Salaries charged = 1,80,000 − 10,000 + 15,000 = **₹1,85,000**; outstanding salaries ₹15,000 (current liability).\n(ii) Rent income = 60,000 − 12,000 = **₹48,000**; rent received in advance ₹12,000 (current liability).\n(iii) Interest income = 8,000 + 2,000 = **₹10,000**; accrued interest ₹2,000 (current asset).\n(iv) Insurance for 3 months (Jan to Mar) = 14,400 × 3/12 = **₹3,600** charged; prepaid ₹10,800 (current asset).\n(v) Depreciation = 15% of 1,60,000 = **₹24,000**; machinery shown at 1,60,000 − 24,000 = **₹1,36,000**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Priya's boutique shows debtors ₹40,000, bad debts ₹1,500 and a provision for doubtful debts of ₹2,500 in its trial balance. The adjustments are: further bad debts ₹1,000; the provision is to be 5% on debtors; interest on capital at 6% on the opening capital of ₹2,00,000; interest on drawings ₹1,800.\n(i) Find the new provision for doubtful debts.\n(ii) Find the total charge to profit and loss for bad debts and provision.\n(iii) What is the net effect of the two interest items on the proprietor's capital?", a: "(i) New provision = 5% of (40,000 − 1,000) = 5% of 39,000 = **₹1,950**.\n(ii) Charge = bad debts 1,500 + further bad debts 1,000 + new provision 1,950 − old provision 2,500 = **₹1,950**.\n(iii) Interest on capital = 6% of 2,00,000 = ₹12,000 is added to capital; interest on drawings ₹1,800 is deducted. Net increase in capital = 12,000 − 1,800 = **₹10,200**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
